/*!
\page qtbridges-csharp-getting-started.html
\title Getting Started with Qt Quick C#
\brief A quick walkthrough to get you started with Qt Quick C#.

This tutorial shows how to create, run, and modify a Qt Quick C# application.

\section1 Prerequisites

\list
    \li Visual Studio 2022 or newer with .NET desktop development and
        Desktop development with C++ workloads installed.
    \li .NET SDK 8 or newer.
\endlist

\note Works currently on Windows x64 only, subject to the available Qt runtime
in this package.

\section1 Create a Qt Quick C# application

To create a Qt Quick C# application in Visual Studio:

\list 1
    \li Open Visual Studio 2022 and create a new project.
    \li Select "Console App" and click "Next".
    \li Name the project "MyFirstQtQuickApp" and click "Create".
    \li In the "Additional information" dialog, select ".NET 8.0" as the
        framework and click "Create".
    \li Once the project is created, open Visual Studio 2022's NuGet Package
        Manager: in Solution Explorer, right-click on the project and select
        "Manage NuGet Packages".
    \li Search for "QtGroup.Qt.Bridge.CSharp" and install the latest version of the package.
\endlist

\section1 Add a QML View

To display a user interface, you'll need to add a QML view to the application.

To add a QML view to the application:

\list 1
\li Right-click on the project in Solution Explorer, select "Add" > "New Item...".
\li Select "Text File", name it "Main.qml", and click "Add".
\li Replace the content of "Main.qml" with the following QML code:
    \code
    import QtQuick
    import QtQuick.Controls
    import QtQuick.Layouts

    ApplicationWindow {
        id: window; width: 220; height: 240; visible: true; title: "Names"

        NameList { id: names }

        ColumnLayout {
            anchors.fill: parent

            TextField {
                Layout.fillWidth: true; leftPadding: 10; focus: true;
                placeholderText: "Enter a name"
                onAccepted: {
                    let name = text.trim();
                    if (name)
                        names.add(name);
                    clear();
                }
            }

            ListView {
                model: names
                Layout.fillWidth: true; Layout.fillHeight: true; clip: true
                delegate: Text {
                    required property string item
                    text: item; leftPadding: 10
                }
            }
        }
    }
    \endcode
\li Edit the C# code in "Program.cs" to load the QML file:
    \code
    using Qt.Quick;

    namespace Tutorial
    {
        internal class Program
        {
            static void Main(string[] args)
            {
                Qml.LoadFromRootModule("MainWindow");
                Qml.WaitForExit();
            }
        }
    }
    \endcode
\endlist

\section1 Create the C# backend

\list 1
\li Right-click on the project in Solution Explorer, select "Add" > "Class...".
\li Name the class "NameList.cs" and click "Add".
\li Replace the content of "NameList.cs" with the following C# code:
    \code
    using Qt.Bridge.Models;

    namespace Tutorial
    {
        public class NameList : ListModel<string>
        {
            private List<string> Names { get; } = new();

            public void Add(string name)
            {
                BeginInsertItems(Names.Count, Names.Count);
                Names.Add(name);
                EndInsertItems();
            }

            public override string Data(int index)
            {
                if (index >= Names.Count)
                    return null;
                return Names[index];
            }

            public override int ItemCount() => Names.Count;
        }
    }
    \endcode
\endlist

\section1 Run the application

To run the application, press F5 or click the "Start" button.

\section1 Next steps

Explore more about Qt Quick C# by visiting the following resources:

\list
    \li \l{Using project and item templates}
    \li \l{Debugging the generated code}
    \li \l{Building Qt Bridges C\\# module from source}
\endlist

*/
